[Chapter "Advance Variation"]
[Line "Short System (4.Nf3)"]
[Opening "Caro-Kann Defense: Advance Variation, Short Variation"]

{White grabs space with 3.e5 and develops quietly. Black gets the bishop out, then attacks the pawn chain.} 1. e4 c6 {Prepares ...d5, so Black can challenge the centre with a pawn while the c8 bishop keeps its diagonal open.} 2. d4 d5 {The point of the Caro-Kann: hit e4 straight away, backed up by the c6 pawn.} 3. e5 {White gains space and closes the centre.} 3... Bf5 {The reason for ...c6 instead of ...e6: the bishop gets outside the pawn chain before it is locked in.} 4. Nf3 e6 {Now ...e6 is safe: the "bad" bishop is already developed.} 5. Be2 c5 {Hit d4, the base of White's pawn chain. This is Black's main source of counterplay in the Advance.} 6. O-O Nc6 {Another attacker on d4.} 7. c3 cxd4 {Release the tension; White has to recapture with the c-pawn.} 8. cxd4 Nge7 {The knight heads for f5, where it hits d4 and can't easily be chased away. It also leaves f6 free for ...f6 later.} *
